I am not familiar with the _doprnt() routine, however I had a need to perform
a similar function.  I wanted to write a function of the form:

fperror(fmt,args) char *fmt; int args;
{
}

which would function much like printf().  However, depending on system
parameters, would perform various logging of the error messages.  The obvious
way would be to use sprintf() to format the message and then pass the buffer
to my routine.  This is what i origionally did.  However, it made the code
somewhat unreadable.

Then I realized that the vsprintf() routine provided with my c library
took its parameters in the same form as &args of above. The function then
bacame:

fperror(fmt,args) char *fmt; int args;
{
	char msgbuff[128];

	vsprintf(msgbuff,fmt,&args);	/* format args into msgbuff based on fmt */

	.
	.
	.
	process the formatted arguments.
}

This is very likely not portable.  However, on a machine where this method
works, I would much rather have my parameters processed by the SAME routine
as other sprintf's, printf's, and fprintf's.  And save a home-brew routine
for other systems.


